<h2>Reducing and Enlarging by Specifying a Ratio</h2>
<p>Use the following procedure to reduce or enlarge the print image by 
 specifying a percentage between 25 and 400% (adjustable in steps of 1%).</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Open the printing preferences dialog box after 
 creating a document in an application.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the <span class="Term-Soft">Basic</span> menu 
 on the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed Settings</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>Select <span class="Term-Soft">Zoom</span> or <span class="Term-Soft">Directional Magnification</span> in the <span class="Term-Soft">Reduce/Enlarge:</span> 
 list.</p></li>
<li><p>If <span class="Term-Soft">Zoom</span> is selected, 
 specify a reduction/enlargement percentage by using the spin box of <span class="Term-Soft">Zoom:</span>. This ratio can be adjusted in steps of 1%.<br/>
 If <span class="Term-Soft">Directional Magnification</span> is selected, 
 specify horizontal and vertical ratios separately using the spin boxes 
 of <span class="Term-Soft">Vertical:</span> and <span class="Term-Soft">Horizontal:</span>.</p></li>
<li><p>After making any other necessary settings, click 
 <span class="Term-OS">OK</span> to close the printing preferences dialog 
 box.</p></li>
<li><p>Start printing.</p></li>
</ol>
<h2>Reducing and Enlarging to Match the Specified Paper Size</h2>
<p>Use the following procedure to reduce or enlarge a document to fit it 
 to a specified paper size. The enlargement/reduction ratio is automatically 
 determined based on the original size and paper size you specify. This 
 method is useful when reducing or enlarging from and to standard paper 
 sizes, for example, reducing an A3 document onto A4 paper.</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Open the printing preferences dialog box after 
 creating a document in an application.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the <span class="Term-Soft">Frequently Used 
 Settings</span> tab or the <span class="Term-Soft">Basic</span> menu of 
 the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed Settings</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>Make sure that the original orientation and document 
 size of the <span class="Term-Soft">Document Size:</span> list are identical 
 to those set in the application.<br/>
 Usually the application settings are reflected here, but some settings 
 may not depend on the application. If different, change these settings 
 so that they are identical to those set from the application.</p></li>
</ol>
<div class="help-step-extra">
<ul>
<li><p class="Normal-indent">Click the <span class="Term-Soft">&lt;&lt; Collapse list</span> button if the <span class="Term-Soft">Document 
 Size:</span> list on the <span class="Term-Soft">Frequently Used Settings</span> 
 tab is not displayed.</p></li>
</ul>
</div>
<ol start="4">
<li><p>If the <span class="Term-Soft">Frequently Used Settings</span> 
 tab is open, make sure that <span class="Term-Soft">Fit to Print Size</span> 
 in <span class="Term-Soft">Reduce/Enlarge:</span> is displayed under the 
 print image. If not, select <span class="Term-Soft">Fit to Print Size</span> 
 in the <span class="Term-Soft">Reduce/Enlarge:</span> list on the <span class="Term-Soft">Basic</span> menu of the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed 
 Settings</span> tab.<br/>
 If the <span class="Term-Soft">Basic</span> menu of the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed 
 Settings</span> tab is open, select <span class="Term-Soft">Fit to Print 
 Size</span> in the <span class="Term-Soft">Reduce/Enlarge:</span> list. 
 </p></li>
<li><p>Select the physical paper size you want to print 
 on from the <span class="Term-Soft">Print On:</span> list.</p></li>
<li><p>After making any other necessary settings, click 
 <span class="Term-OS">OK</span> to close the printing preferences dialog 
 box.</p></li>
<li><p>Start printing.</p></li>
</ol>
